    {
      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "To solve the problem, we need to determine the number of distinct equilateral triangles that can be formed with vertices from the set \\(\\{A_1, A_2, \\ldots, A_9\\}\\) on a regular nine-sided polygon \\(A_1A_2A_3A_4A_5A_6A_7A_8A_9\\).\n\nFirst, let's understand the properties of equilateral triangles in a regular polygon. For an equilateral triangle to be formed, the vertices must be separated by an angle of \\(120^\\circ\\) or \\(240^\\circ\\) (i.e., every \\(3\\) or \\(7\\) vertices respectively).\n\nLet's consider the vertices of the polygon in a clockwise order:\n\\[ A_1, A_2, A_3, A_4, A_5, A_6, A_7, A_8, A_9. \\]\n\nWe will examine the possible sets of vertices for an equilateral triangle.\n\n### Case 1: Vertices of type \\(3\\) (120 degrees apart)\nWe need to find sets of three vertices that are positioned such that the central angle between any two consecutive vertices is \\(120^\\circ\\). These vertices can be paired as follows:\n\\[ (A_1, A_4, A_7), (A_2, A_5, A_8), (A_3, A_6, A_9). \\]\n\nEach pair forms an equilateral triangle. There are 3 such sets.\n\n### Case 2: Vertices of type \\(7\\) (240 degrees apart)\nSimilarly, we need to find sets of three vertices that are positioned such that the central angle between any two consecutive vertices is \\(240^\\circ\\). These vertices can be paired as follows:\n\\[ (A_1, A_8, A_4), (A_2, A_9, A_5), (A_3, A_6, A_7). \\]\n\nEach pair also forms an equilateral triangle. There are 3 such sets.\n\n### Total number of distinct equilateral triangles\nAdding the number of sets from both cases, we get:\n\\[ 3 + 3 = 6. \\]\n\nThus, the number of distinct equilateral triangles in the plane of the polygon that have at least two vertices in the set \\(\\{A_1, A_2, \\ldots, A_9\\}\\) is \\(\\boxed{6}\\).",
